00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:03.030
Another useful feature of
Excel is the ability to display

00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:04.500
information in chart format.

00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:07.500
Charts can be useful because
they allow you to present

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:09.150
information in a graphical format.

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:13.320
So people can see at a glance, the
variation in sales or variation in income.

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:18.505
And if there's any concerns they can
see the difference between the best

00:00:18.535 --> 00:00:20.415
month sales or the worst month sales.

00:00:20.905 --> 00:00:23.395
So this can allow people to see
that rather than just looking at

00:00:23.395 --> 00:00:27.325
data on a worksheet, seeing it
in a graphical representation can

00:00:27.325 --> 00:00:31.135
point out things that may not be
as clear within the data itself.

00:00:32.034 --> 00:00:34.945
So the first thing you need to do is
you need to select the information that

00:00:34.945 --> 00:00:36.625
you're going to create the chart from.

00:00:36.775 --> 00:00:40.195
And what I'm going to do here is I'm
going to create a chart to show the

00:00:40.195 --> 00:00:42.025
total sales for all the products.

00:00:42.885 --> 00:00:45.945
So the first thing I need to do is
I need to select the product data.

00:00:46.365 --> 00:00:50.955
So if I click in the product cell,
A1, and click and hold and drag

00:00:50.955 --> 00:00:53.715
that down to "weekend experience".

00:00:54.075 --> 00:00:58.515
Now because the information that I need
to create the chart is not alongside

00:00:58.515 --> 00:01:02.835
each other, so there are quite a lot of
columns between the product and total

00:01:02.835 --> 00:01:07.635
sales, to select non-adjacent columns,
I need to hold down the control key.

00:01:07.875 --> 00:01:13.020
So if I press and hold the control key,
select the total sales heading, and then

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:18.810
drag that down to the weekend experience
figure, and then release the control key.

00:01:19.289 --> 00:01:22.590
You can see that I have now
selected those two columns.

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:25.860
So from there, we need
to create the chart.

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:30.600
So to do that, we go up to the insert tab
and I click on the insert tab and we need

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:33.300
to navigate along to the chart section.

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:38.070
Now there are various icons within
here, so you can add specific

00:01:38.070 --> 00:01:41.130
types of charts so that one there
would be for a column or bar chart,

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:43.350
line chart, pie chart, and so on.

00:01:43.889 --> 00:01:47.970
For this instance, I'm just going to
select the recommended chart icon.

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:52.725
So if I click on that, and that
brings up the insert chart dialog box.

00:01:53.455 --> 00:01:57.065
Now a good thing here is it's given me
a graphical representation of what the

00:01:57.065 --> 00:01:58.574
chart will look like when I select it.

00:01:58.595 --> 00:02:04.714
So that would be a bar chart, a pie
chart, a column chart, and a funnel chart.

00:02:05.015 --> 00:02:07.895
And the one I'm going to select
for this is a column chart.

00:02:08.315 --> 00:02:14.010
So once I've selected that if I select
OK, that then now creates the chart.

00:02:14.450 --> 00:02:18.200
So we've now created a chart so the
total sales for each of the products.

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:21.269
So you've got the one hour, two
hour, half the experience, full day

00:02:21.290 --> 00:02:23.630
experience and weekend experience.

00:02:23.839 --> 00:02:29.420
And just to check, you can see if you
look at the actual data, you can see that

00:02:29.510 --> 00:02:31.279
that is the data that's been selected.

00:02:31.279 --> 00:02:35.269
It has been highlighted by bounding
boxes around the information.

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:38.510
So once we've got that, we
